A||N||19||FieroJoe||Finally Got My Rims Done!||||1||
Z||000000||FieroJoe||01-14-2001||09:30 PM||||After what seemed like an eternity, I finally finished up refinishing and polishing my 14" hi-tech rims.  It took forever, and was very frustrating at many times.  I hope it will be worth it, once I see what they look like on the car.  Oh well, that's one project of the myriad of others to scratch off my winter Fiero list!<P> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/shinyrims.JPG"> <BR>||||reg||1
Z||000001||terryk||01-14-2001||09:39 PM||||I'm doing mine (Grand AM 16") and keep coming up with scratches that won't sand out. 220-400-600-1000-1500. Clean them up and they have fine scratches. GM rims contain some alloy of extra-terrestrial metals from what I guess.<P>The compounds have no affect on the scratches at all. Emery (gray), Tripolt (red), rouge (white).<BR> <BR>They polish up nicely, but those scratches are there.<P>Any clues?<p>[This message has been edited by terryk (edited 01-14-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000002||FieroJoe||01-14-2001||09:56 PM||||If I had more time I would have gone finer, all the way to 1500, like you did, but here's the lowdown:<P>180-220-600 sanding<P>Dico brand Tripoli, brown<P>Some wierd compound that we have about 30 pounds of in my barn<P>Dico brand Jewler's Rouge, red<P>and then just got done tonight with Mother's Mag and Aluminum Wheel Polish.  <P>Even after the Jewler's Rouge, I could still see some scratches even from the compound.  It takes a lot of elbow grease, but eventually you can get rid of them with the mother's polish.  I have also been told theres a similar product called "Flitz".  They said it works better than Mothers, but I can not attest to that.<P>You can still see some scratches on mine, if you look close enough.  If I had more time I would have done a much better job, but for my first try, I'm pretty proud of them.<P>Anyway, Good luck!<P>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/mothers.JPG">  <p>[This message has been edited by FieroJoe (edited 01-14-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000003||sqoach||01-14-2001||10:04 PM||||they look really nice fierojoe! good job.<BR>i want mine to look that shiny, but just always have stuff to do for some reason. i do have a question though. is there a layer of something on the wheels? i noticed one of my wheels flaking.<P>------------------<BR>sQoaCh<BR>sqoach@yahoo.com<BR>        <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/mysig_2.JPG"> ||||reg||14
Z||000004||FieroJoe||01-14-2001||10:11 PM||||Yep there is.  It's clearcoat that you see flaking.  And it was the worst part to try to get off.  Use Permatex "Spray on Gasket Remover"  It will remove all the clear coat, and also the gray paint.  Most will recommend repainting the gray if you remove it, to give the rims some depth.  So, if you dont want to have to repaint them, you can use semi-paste paint stripper, and steel wool.  Email me and I can tell you more if you want.||||reg||1
Z||000005||terryk||01-14-2001||10:45 PM||||Ok, pretty much the same thing I'm doing. I think I need to be more agressive at 400 and up.||||reg||1
Z||000006||RNGCRSH||01-14-2001||11:21 PM||||If you want to have a mirror finish, you need to go up to a much finner grit before using the tripoli. You need to at least go up to 1500, if you can find it 2400~3600 grit then use the tripoli and rouge. I used to remove scrathes from F-18 cockpit canopies with emory cloth that went up to a 15000 grit befor using a cream polish that enhanced it to 25000. I use a similar product now for my pen making business Its called micro mesh. This stuff isn't cheap but the fine srathes will be gone. If you use a soft rubber sponge behind your paper you will reduce the number of deep scratches the sand paper will leave behind.||||reg||1
Z||000007||terryk||01-14-2001||11:23 PM||||Ok, pretty much the same thing I'm doing. I think I need to be more agressive at 400 and up.||||reg||1
Z||000008||Eric||01-14-2001||11:56 PM||||When I polished my rims I used stainless polish after 600 grit.  It did a lot of the work for me, and I don't think I even needed to use the finer compound polishes.  I guess there are a few little scratches still in the aluminum, but hey, they're wheels.||||reg||1
Z||000009||FieroMaster88||01-15-2001||12:06 AM||||Good work Joe!!!  they look nice!  Now, I have some rims on my indy that could use some work...... <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/FieroMaster88.gif">  <BR>James Essar<BR>AOL IM Name: FieroMaster86<BR>88 Coupe 30,000 miles<BR>84 Indy Fiero (restoration Project)||||reg||1
