Z||000011||overlord||01-15-2001||02:18 AM||||they look good.<BR>im currently on wheel #3 of 5.<BR>sanpaper wize i  went:<BR>220<BR>400<BR>600<BR>800<BR>1500<BR>then polishing compounds. and repainted the black parts chrome (removing that paint took forever)||||reg||1
Z||000012||Homicidal||01-15-2001||04:36 AM||||GOOD?!?<P>Those are friggin GORGEOUS!  Absolutely gorgeous job!<P>I attempted to do this, but I've never had time to finish.  I am thinking of just paying to have them done.<P>How much would it be for 4 rims to be done like that??||||reg||1
Z||000013||Phil||01-15-2001||08:10 AM||||Has anybody played with having their finished rim powder coated with clear?||||reg||1
Z||000014||FieroJoe||01-15-2001||10:19 AM||||I would have to say that to pay to have them done would be big bucks.  The labor cost is where they will get ya.  I spent at least 20 hours total, possibly more.<P>I dont know about powder coating either.  I was going to clear coat them, but you would have to use a hardener with the acrylic enamel.  The hardener is a must, and it also makes the paint more brittle.  To keep the rims shiny, I'll just polish them with the mothers when they get dirty from road dust.||||reg||1
Z||000015||2xsess||01-15-2001||12:59 PM||||I've heard that there is a company that refinishes these wheels for $300 a set. i don't have any contact info as SDM1964 is the one who told me about it and he is in Ft Polk LA right now.<P>Looking sweet Joe.<P>------------------<BR>Jeff<BR>AOL IM : Only2xsess<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/My_car_(large)sig_1.JPG"> ||||reg||1
Z||000016||Raydar||01-15-2001||01:00 PM||||FieroJoe, Those look great! Looks like a bunch of work went into them. (Been there.)<P>For anyone interested...<BR>When I polished mine, I documented the procedure. Check Darklord's site.<BR> <A HREF="http://www.fieroproject.com" TARGET=_blank>www.fieroproject.com</A>  <BR>Click on the "Modifications" Link, then the exterior section.<P>The most difficult part for me also was stripping the paint. Thought about just clear-coating the centers, instead of repainting. <BR>Maybe next time.    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/eek.gif"><P>    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Wheel.jpg">    <P><BR><p>[This message has been edited by Raydar (edited 01-15-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000017||theogre||01-15-2001||08:02 PM||||On some of the larger surfaces try a random orbit sander. This is less likely to dig a big scratch, especially in the early stages. One of those wierd little finishing sanders like a Mouse might help. Those things are made for tight work and odd shapes.||||reg||1
Z||000018||terryk||01-15-2001||08:20 PM||||I've finally got two done. Here's what I learned.<P>1. There is a lot of detail between stripping the clear and the final polish that seems to be left out.<P>2. Use only hi-quality sandpaper (3M). Only 3M stayed together and continued cutting and cut tons better. I tried several brands. <P>3. Use WD-40 instead of water (recommended by 3M). It stays in place and keeps the paper from being clogged.<P>Once I learned those lessons, here are the finer detail.<P>4. If you want the surface to be scratch free, clean the rims thoroughly between each stage (each grit if sandpaper and each stage of polish). Use paint thinner, not denatured alcohol, otherwise you will leave marks behind that standout on the first polishing level and you start all over again. And again, and again .... <P>This is even more important between polishes. The coarser grits will get into tiny grooves and channels (PONTIAC) and get sucked back out by the pad on the finer steps and leave blemishes. Clean, clean, clean, clean......<P>5. Mothers Aluminum polish is somewhere between emery and tripoli in grit. It's certainly coarser than jewler's rouge. If you follow the normal three steps of emery, tripoli, rouge, don't use Mothers, it dulls the finish. <P>6. Use an obital or circular sander otherwise your arms will die. I am now armless and typing with my nose.<P>Here are my steps:<BR>240 for the machine marks, clean and check.<BR>240 for the machine marks you missed, clean and check.<BR>320, clean and check.<BR>400, clean and check.<BR>600, clean and check.<BR>1000, check and check and check and check.<BR>1500, clean and check.<BR>Emery compound, clean, clean, clean, check.<BR>Emery compound again, clean and check.<BR>Triopli, clean and check. Watch for Emery being brought back into to the buffing from grooves in the metal. Looks like bright blemishes.<BR>Jeweler's Rouge, clean and check, again and again.<P>Figure 8-10 hours/wheel and about $50 in materials. <P>I'm very happy with the results. <P><p>[This message has been edited by terryk (edited 01-15-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000019||danny boy||01-15-2001||09:01 PM|||| They look good.Real good.  <BR>  I used a product called Peeler to get the clearcoat off mine. I got it at Napa.Spray on, let it sit,Blow it off with compressed air.Worked great.<P>------------------<BR>Stealths faster||||reg||14
